<<~ kanawai uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119`>>

! Founding Runbook ~ one household walk

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-rite`>>

!! 1 · What founding makes

A node founded by one operator stands as a SEED. A Nexus stands when two operators each seat their own key in a fresh charter (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus`>>). One act founds the household's node, the Stable founding act (`lares vessel found --hearth`): under one human route it mints the custody, the place and persona zero, and a PRIVATE seed charter with one seat at threshold one, whose next key rests on a cold-key card; persona zero's face binds that charter's Nexus AID. No keyring rides it. The node then seeds its corpus from the tracked bags. No prior CRDT document needs to survive. Each operator founds their own node on their own machine. A browser counts as a leaf of its human's Lararium; each device mints its own key, one face per device, and no device receives a seed.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/fresh-state`>>

!! Fresh state

A fresh founding needs only a fresh house root (`LARES_HOUSE`) and the workspace the operator names at `/workspace` (#/prepare). For an older installation, move its homes aside by hand before founding again, as the stable founding road directs. No CLI pave or wipe command stands.

''Do'': prepare the first operator's fresh house root (`LARES_HOUSE`, bound at `/lar`) and name the workspace `/workspace` binds (#/prepare), then follow this walk.

''See'': the repo carries the corpus and genesis; the new house root holds no prior vessel home.

''Proves'': founding needs no old CRDT document, migration, clear, or rebirth.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/prepare`>>

!! 2 · Prepare the machine

All code runs in Docker. The host keeps the shell shim, hooks, mounts, editor, and custody copies. Use a local filesystem for every mount; keep the house root outside the repository and separate from `/workspace`, which binds the workspace the operator names. The vessel image carries code and genesis, never household state. The app receives no Docker socket.

Set `LARES_HOUSE` to the first operator's house root (an absolute path; its `hearth/`, `sensoriums/`, `projections/`, `library/` and `config/` bind at `/lar` in the container) and `LARES_WORKSPACE` to the workspace the house lays canon from and projects into, which holds tracked `bags/` and receives `wikis/`. Export both paths in this shell before running the host preparation commands, or name them in `~/.lares/lares-surface.env`, which rests outside every bind (the checkout carries none; the shim refuses an env file inside one of the house's binds). On Windows, keep every mount inside the WSL distro; a new operator there walks <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/wsl2/new-hearth`>>, which strings the host runbook, this walk's steps 2, 5 and 6, the keep and the MCP seat into one order, with the house root at `~/.lares` (the root the restore in 11a names). Keep a terminal attached for founding and custody credentials; the CLI refuses a credential act without a TTY.

''Do'': build the product image and create the house root.

```sh
export LARES_HOUSE="$HOME/lares-house"
export LARES_WORKSPACE="$PWD"
mkdir -p "$LARES_HOUSE"
tools/lares-container.sh build
```

''Workspaces receive the house's projection.'' The operator names each workspace, and the house projects bags, wikis and rendered artifacts into it; `LARES_WORKSPACE` names the one the vessel binds at `/workspace`, where it lays canon from and where its disk mirror writes canon edits back. For the Kahu cabal that workspace names the repo checkout: the working surface, with a live Lararium projecting into it and canon edits landing in the tracked `bags/` and `wikis/` as source-control write-back. No carve-out, no flag and no required canon clone stand; another operator names a corpus directory of their own. `node tools/house-canon.mjs` stays as a tool where a separate pinned clone serves a house, never a requirement.

''Read-back follows descent.'' The house remembers the commit it last laid canon from. A fast-forward of the workspace's commit flows in with no ingest act; a fork (a branch switch, a rebase, a reset) holds the house's canon and asks one plain question: take the workspace's canon, or re-project the house's over it? Uncommitted edits stand as hints to the Confluence, and a vanished file waits as a pending removal until a person answers. What notices the workspace's commit moving stands owed (no watcher runs by default); until it lands, `lares vessel lay` reads moved canon by an explicit act.

Run these from the root of the household checkout; the house path shown stands as an example, and any local directory outside the checkout serves. `build` rebuilds the vessel image, every package the vessel runs, from the committed checkout: it runs `docker compose build lares` through the env file (a bare `docker compose build` reads no `lares-surface.env` and refuses on `LARES_HOUSE`) and bakes the checkout's HEAD into the image's revision label; a checkout holding uncommitted tracked changes refuses, so commit first. The shim makes the house's five bound classes before a container stands. Under PowerShell, assign `$env:LARES_HOUSE` and `$env:LARES_WORKSPACE` before calling the `.ps1` shim.

Use `tools/lares-container.sh` on Unix-like hosts or `tools/lares-container.ps1` on Windows. The shims read the same environment or `lares-surface.env` and route CLI work through the image.

''The host `lares` answers through the container house.'' With `LAR_ROOT` unset and `~/.lares/lares-surface.env` standing, the host's own `lares <verb>` hands every vessel verb to the checkout's `tools/lares-container.sh` with the same argv, so `lares up`, `lares vessel read` and `lares tiddler get … --meme --json | jq` work from the WSL shell as the shim lines below do; the exit code and stdout pass through untouched, and no socket crosses the container wall. Host verbs stay on the host: `home`, `repo`, `help` and every `--help`, `wing-of`, `mempalace`, and `meme normalize|check|project <file> --to md`; `lares --help` marks each verb `host`, `vessel` or `mixed`. A credential verb (`vault unlock|bind|unbind`, `found`) refuses by name without a terminal. With neither `LAR_ROOT` nor the env file, a vessel verb refuses in two lines naming both cures. A path argument (`export <dest>`, `import <bundle>`, `vault backup --out <file>`, `ingest --source <dir>`, …) reads from the host, relative paths and `~` included, and crosses in the vessel's spelling: `$LARES_HOUSE/<class>` reads at `/lar/<class>` and `$LARES_WORKSPACE` at `/workspace`; a path already spelled `/lar/…` or `/workspace/…` passes as written, and a host path outside those mounts refuses by name before docker runs, so `lares export "$LARES_HOUSE/config/carry/bundle-one"` and the shim's `/lar/config/carry/bundle-one` name one place. A Ctrl+C or a lone SIGTERM stops the act inside the vessel too: the shim stops the tagged process it exec'd, or the run container it named. A shell that names `LAR_ROOT` (a scratch house, a suite, the container itself) never forwards. Windows runs `lares` inside WSL.

''See'': Docker reports a successful image build; the house root exists and the workspace holds tracked `bags/`.

''Proves'': the host has the image and durable homes required by the compose surface.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/pre-rite`>>

!! 2b · The pre-rite, three acts before the first `found`

Three acts stand between a landed head and the first `found --hearth`. Each one closes a failure the walks measured, and each one waits for the one before it.

# ''Rebuild the image LAST, at the landed head.'' Land every branch the rite needs, then check out that head, and only then run `tools/lares-container.sh build` (under PowerShell: `tools/lares-container.ps1 build`); the shim reads the env file, passes the checkout's HEAD as `GIT_REV` into the image's revision label, and refuses a dirty checkout by name, so the label always names a real commit. An image built before the last landing runs older code than the checkout holds, and nothing else tells the operator. The image carries `org.opencontainers.image.revision`; every stand and restart the shim performs (`up`, the bare `vessel stand`, a re-stand) compares that label to `git rev-parse HEAD` and warns, naming both, when they differ. A warning means the rebuild stays owed: rebuild with `tools/lares-container.sh build`, the command the warning prints, then `up` again. A dirty checkout warns too and `build` refuses it, so commit first.
# ''Move old homes aside by RENAME alone.'' Stop the vessel first (`tools/lares-container.sh down`). `down` asks the standing vessel's census first (`lares vessel census`: saves its sealed rest holds in memory, carriers its disk projector has not settled, and each file a mirror root inside a checkout holds that git never tracked, an owed commit named by path); a clean census stops at once, and any item, or a census that cannot answer (a wedged vessel, an image older than the census door), lists what it learned and stops only after `vault unlock` lets it settle or after `accept-loss` typed on the terminal. A house root or workspace mirror from an earlier walk then gets a new name (`mv ~/lares-house ~/lares-house.before-rite`), never a delete, never an overwrite. The rename keeps the old bytes readable for a repair or a comparison, and the fresh `LARES_HOUSE` starts empty so the founding reads no leftover. Nothing in this runbook removes a home; `tools/lares-container.sh reset` clears `$LARES_HOUSE/hearth` alone, after a typed `re-found`, and leaves the sensoriums, projections, library and config standing.
# ''Check the projection before trusting the layout.'' The canon source (`bags/` under the checkout) sources no `daemon` slug, and the face's working top projects under its face root, `$LARES_HOUSE/projections/faces/<pet name>/wikis/daemon`, never under the checkout's `wikis/daemon/`. After the first `persona new 0`, look for `$LARES_HOUSE/projections/faces/<pet name>/wikis/daemon` and for `.../bags/kahua`; `$LARES_HOUSE/projections/faces/<pet name>/bags/daemon` holds the face daemon canon and its authored memory; a control or house record there (a `bags/control/**`, `bags/persona/**` or `dreamnet/**` path) means a control record rested in a mirrored bag, and the rite stops there. Placement keeps control records off disk: they rest in the vessel control document, which no mirror names. The bag-paths suite pins both placements (`projectionRoots` over this checkout's real `bags/`, and `daemonWorkingMirror`), so a mismatch on a live disk names a stale image first.

''Proves'': the image names the revision the checkout holds, the old bytes rest beside the new home under their own names, and the projection lands where the ruled layout says.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-hands`>>

!! 2a · The operator's hands before the two-hearth day

Each act below waits on a person and a machine; no session performs one. Check each off in order, and read its ''See'' before the next.

# ''The registrar key'': limit it to the one household domain, switch on its API access, and place the two credential files outside both mounts on the always-on node. ''See'': the one-shot issuer completes its first live issuance.
# ''The router'': hand out the node's resolver address to the LAN. ''See'': a LAN device resolves the household names through it.
# ''The node's ports'': 53 (UDP and TCP), 443 (TCP) and 5353 (UDP, mDNS) stand free on the node's LAN interface (`ss -lntup`). ''See'': nothing else listens there.
# ''A WSL2 node'': WSL runs with `networkingMode=mirrored` and `dnsTunneling=false` in `%USERPROFILE%\.wslconfig`, so the node sits on the real LAN; with `firewall=true`, Windows Firewall governs WSL traffic, so inbound allow rules for UDP and TCP 53, TCP 443 and UDP 5353 stand on the private LAN profile. ''See'': the second machine pings the node's LAN address and reaches its port 443.
# ''The devices'': run `tools/capability-probe/` on the tablet and on one phone before browser custody hardens. ''See'': each device's measured row.
# ''One real-LAN discovery run'': mDNS offers its unauthoritative candidates on the household LAN. ''See'': the second machine lists the node.
# ''The second machine'': docker stands, and it pulls the product image by the digest the release manifest names. ''See'': `docker image inspect` shows that digest.
# ''The ember's anchor'': choose a hardware freshness anchor for unattended raises, or accept that every raise asks a person's confirm; the day needs neither, since reach stays LAN only.
# ''The release index'': push and sign it after the last genesis re-bake and the last wire change.
# ''The fresh start'', on both machines: move the old homes aside by rename, never delete (#/fresh-state).
# ''The dress rehearsal'' with synthetic names, every cold-key card read back with `lares nexus seal cold rehearse`; then the ceremony (#/second-operator).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/decisions`>>

!! 3 · Decide names and custody before typing

Choose the household domain and DNS provider; name the host that the household resolver will answer. Choose a private persona label and an outward Handle for each operator. A seat needs no public Handle; choose its private chair label and where its cold-key paper rests. Name the custody holders and separate storage places before the CLI prints recovery cards. Keep domain credentials outside the vessel image, compose inputs, and state mount.

Synthetic examples below use `operator-one`, `Operator One`, and `hearth.home.example.test`. Replace each with household choices. A private label and a public Handle carry separate meanings, even when their text matches.

''Do'': write down the chosen labels, Handle, chair, domain, backup path, and custody destinations.

''See'': each decision names a holder, a location, or a persona act.

''Proves'': the founding commands can use deliberate household values rather than inventing them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/rehearse`>>

!! 4 · Rehearse in an isolated Docker project

The harbor witness gives each leg fresh scratch state and workspace, chooses its own Compose project, and removes only its own containers and scratch directories. It builds the local image if that tag does not exist. Give the project a unique name if another rehearsal already uses the default. Every boot rests at the floor holding no vessel key, so the witness opens each scratch vessel through the test custody door (`tools/unlock-staged-container.sh`); that door refuses any state directory a harness did not stage, and a household vessel opens on a terminal alone.

''Do'': run the harbor container walk.

```sh
HARBOR_PROJECT=founding-rehearsal tools/harbor-walk-witness.sh
```

''See'': `PASS` lines for both legs: the hearth founded before `up` (`found --hearth`, then `up`, the founding face standing open), the floor that holds no key, the unlock that composes a Lararium wearing the face, workspace projection, a place lifted to a hearth after `up` (a face refuses on a place with no seed charter; `found --hearth` lifts it once the vessel stops), and a final `PASS harbor walk`.

''Proves'': the shim routes a TTY-safe founding, persona, and corpus walk through the image under review. This rehearsal creates no keeper state.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/found`>>

!! 5 · Found the first node

One act founds the hearth, before `up`: `found --hearth`. Under ONE human route, typed on the attached terminal (a passphrase, a paper pin, or both), it mints the custody (the vessel key, the serving key and the custody root), the place, and persona zero with its private label and a claim on one chair. It then mints that chair's own seat key and prints two cold-key cards, each asked back before the act goes on: first the PLACE card (the place's NEXT key, which `vessel rotate` reads back to rotate the place), then the chair's NEXT key, asked back before the charter moves. Only then does its one seat sign the inception image through the custody door's `seal.incept`, and the act writes the PRIVATE seed charter (one seat, threshold one, the card's digest committed) and persona zero's face, which binds the charter's Nexus AID: the device edge, the dyad scope and the face's cabal sentinel all name that AID. No keyring rides the act. A run that stops anywhere runs again and completes; a card that never reads back writes no charter and no face (`CardReadBackRefused`). `--name` carries persona zero's private label; `--chair` labels the chair privately.

''The card.'' Each prints 64 hex characters and a checksum in groups of four, with its purpose, the chair's label, its generation and a short charter fingerprint. Write it onto paper, then type it back; three readings that never match refuse. ''Paper alone holds a card or a paper sheet: never paste, type, photograph or screenshot a card line or a sheet word into a chat, an AI session, a note or a password manager.'' A line that reached any other surface counts as burned, so found again at a fresh house root before anything lands. After the last read-back, clear the terminal's scrollback (`clear && printf '\033[3J'`), so no later paste carries a card line. No copy rests on the node, and the act's answer carries the card's digest and confirm phrase alone. A live card prints on a terminal and never into `--json`, a redirect or a log; on a marked scratch root alone the read-back stands in, and the JSON answer carries the staged card's code for the harness.

''Do'': on the first operator's machine, replace the synthetic values and run each line in order.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh found --hearth --name 'operator-one' --chair 'first-chair'
tools/lares-container.sh persona new 0 --name 'operator-one' --handle 'Operator One'
tools/lares-container.sh up
tools/lares-container.sh vessel lay --apply --yes
tools/lares-container.sh vessel read
tools/lares-container.sh persona list --json
tools/lares-container.sh nexus seal show
tools/lares-container.sh meme list --recipe default --json
```

The second line declares persona zero's public Handle; skip it for a face that keeps none. The label and the Handle land on the sealed records alone, before `up` or on a standing vessel: a face's persona plane rests in the clear on disk, so no name rides onto it until its rest seals (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/owed`>>).

The host shim starts the container at `up`. The vessel stands holding no vessel key, so `up` then runs `vault unlock` on the attached terminal: type the custody passphrase, and the vessel opens its key, opens the serving key through the vessel key's own entry, and composes with what the keys open. With no pūlama, every restart returns the vessel to the floor; the shim unlocks again after each restart it performs, and with no terminal attached it names the command (`tools/lares-container.sh vault unlock`). With a lit pūlama and an admitted image, a restart wakes standing with its lend (the lit house, below). `tools/lares-container.sh vault lock` drops the key by hand. A face created after `up` triggers the shim's automatic restart when the CLI reports `restand`; the operator never types a restart flag.

''The lit house.'' With `LARES_PULAMA=1` in the environment, the pūlama stands beside the vessel and keeps the faces' keys lit across every rebuild and restart (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/the-worn-house#/the-pulama`>>). Once the faces exist, the operator lights them at the pūlama by their own hand on a real terminal, admits the standing image naming the set of faces lent to the node, and unlocks by the pūlama:

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh pulama light          # the operator's own route lights the faces at the pūlama
tools/lares-container.sh pulama admit 0 1 2    # the standing image's admission names the set lent to the node
tools/lares-container.sh vault unlock          # gathers no route: the vessel dials its pūlama and draws its lend
tools/lares-container.sh pulama status
```

The pūlama lends the lit faces the admission names and names any admitted face still waiting on its light; each session (the MCP seat, a CLI run) wears one face inside the lend, and the vessel wears none. A route typed at the vessel opens nothing. A founding, a ward act and a backup's proof each ask a human route, so a vessel standing by a pūlama refuses them `needs-the-human`. A kūkulu (`lares rite kukulu`) re-stands a rebuilt vessel under one kūkulu lei per lent face, each strung by that face's own hand, and its landing admits the new image.

''A place that stands already lifts the same way.'' Stop the vessel (`tools/lares-container.sh vessel stop`), run `found --hearth` over the standing place, then `up`. A face on a place with no seed charter refuses by name and names the act. Bare `found` still founds a place with no face (a herm's rite). `lares fleet join` carries a joiner through offer, request, answer and land.

''The lay feeds canon through one road.'' `lay` lays every disk mirror back into the layer it mirrors; import carries a bundle onto this hearth's faces by pet-name. Each holding whose canon source stands on disk (its dir under the canon source the daemon reads; a private plane never) takes `CREATE`, which converges on the bag that stands and mints nothing over it, then the diff-gated `INGEST` into `bags/<slug>`; no name list picks the road, so `crossroads`, `sdm` and `lares` ride it alike. The lay's canon road never writes a wiki's working layer and never walks `LOAD` + `MOVE`; a working layer takes only its own mirror back, the wiki's ONE workbench folder (`<projections>/realms/<realm pet name>/wikis/<slug>`; `wikis/<slug>` beside a sourced wiki's canon), once for its document, at the wear of a face that holds a writer's seat there. The recipe stack alone decides what shows, so a working copy keeps shadowing the canon under it; where the lay moved canon past the record a copy forked from, it warns "canon is fresher than the working copy above it" with the count and the first eight titles, and nothing writes (the daemon's `fresher-canon` read lists them all; remove a copy with the ordinary verbs to take the fresher canon). A working-only title never promotes, and a file gone from disk stands pending at the shore. The settle word waits while pages keep settling and stops only after two minutes with none; a stall prints one ⚠ and `lares shore settle --bag <bag> --pending` finishes it without a re-seed. A branch switch that drops files floods the shore with pending removals (a wave tombstones no carrier); the lay counts them per bag and prints the one batch answer, `lares shore remove --all --count <n> --bag <bag>`.

''The lay leaves the checkout as it stood.'' A carrier whose bytes say what its records render stands unchanged, whatever framing a hand gave it, so a first `vessel lay` writes no tracked file and `git status` in the checkout reads clean. The unlock adds no untracked file either: the records the daemon seeds from source never project (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection#/bag-roles`>>), so `wikis/` receives a file only where a person writes one. A later change to a record writes that one carrier in its canonical render.

''See'': the act prints `the hearth stands: nexus-<sha>`, the seed charter at one seat and threshold one, PRIVATE, the seat's fingerprint, and persona zero's edge bound to this hearth. `vessel lay` names each holding under `/workspace/bags` and ends `6/6 holding(s) converged` (the count follows the holdings the checkout carries); the two infrastructure holdings ride the diff-gated ingest, and every other holding walks the island's named road: the act creates the bag, loads the carriers onto that wiki's working layer, then moves each landed record into canon, since a write lands on a working layer and canon takes it by promotion (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection#/bag-roles`>>); a second run submits nothing for a bag that already matches. `vessel read` reports the live node; `persona list` marks index zero active; `nexus seal show` reads SEED with one seated key; the daemon recipe list returns seeded wiki entries.

''Proves'': one act founds the hearth's custody, place, persona zero and seed charter, the vessel lays its corpus from the tracked bags, and it serves as the first operator's live node.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/reserve`>>

!! 6 · Establish custody

Keep a recovery route for the persona root and for the seed charter. The CLI prints one-time recovery material at the acts that mint it. Write each card onto its intended medium and move it to the holder and place chosen in step 3. Never treat a displayed secret as a backup until the human has copied it and checked that copy.

''Do'': inspect the vault and the seal, rehearse the card, then cut persona zero's backup bundle.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh vault status
tools/lares-container.sh nexus seal show
tools/lares-container.sh nexus seal cold rehearse
tools/lares-container.sh vault backup --face 0 --out /lar/config/face-0.bundle
```

The seed's seat holds its own key, sealed on the node, and its NEXT key rests on the card alone. Rehearse the card before the paper leaves the room: `nexus seal cold rehearse` asks for one or more cards on the terminal (a blank line skips a chair), reads each against the digests the head committed, names the chair each would roll, and moves nothing. A card that answers no digest refuses by its place in the typing and never by a byte of its code. The charter grows or rolls only by the roll its card arms (`nexus seal rotate`, which asks each card back the same way); the quorum rite (`nexus rite quorum`) refuses on a hearth, `hearth-bound`, since a re-seat would mint another AID and orphan every relation bound to the first (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/nexus-scale`>>).

''The backup bundle carries one face.'' `vault backup --face <N> --out <file>` runs on the standing vessel, unlocked, with a session wearing that face; under a pūlama it asks a human route and refuses `needs-the-human`. It asks the vessel's own route again, then a NEW route for the bundle (a passphrase typed twice, or a paper sheet read back), and writes the file (`/lar/config` binds `$LARES_HOUSE/config`, which a reset never clears, so the host reads the file there); it never writes over a standing one. The bundle carries the face's root with its own lines, its key history and its content keys, and no key of any seat; the serving tree stays on its hearth. Move the file off the machine. Its memory section holds keys alone: the sealed bytes they open must survive elsewhere, and on a production vessel that section stands empty until the wire cutover opens a session signer (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-cut`>>).

''A restore brings the face back as a recovery act'' onto a fresh hearth: found it first (`found --hearth`, which stands its own persona zero), then, on the standing vessel, `vault restore <file> --as 1`. The restored face takes a new seat under its fixed prefix and binds that hearth's seed charter. It lands at the handle-index its bundle named only where no root stands there; persona zero holds index zero, so a persona-zero bundle takes `--as <N>` at a free index and lands beside persona zero, never as it (`V5` of the roadmap's loops). `vault restore <file> --memory` lands the bundle's content keys once the vessel wears that face (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore`>>).

The credential prompts need the attached terminal. The vessel key seals the keyhive archive, each face's veil archive and each face's anchors where they rest, and the custody slot tree travels with them. For the whole vessel, stop it, then make one consistent copy of the whole house root (`LARES_HOUSE`: the hearth with its custody slots and relation carriers, and the sensoriums, projections, library and config beside it); that copy plus the custody passphrase (or paper pin) restores the vessel whole.

''See'': `vault status` reports held slots, names each sealed record, and reads the serving tree among the records that rest bare by law; `nexus seal show` names the one seat and the head; the rehearsal names the chair the card would roll; the backup prints which bags and versions the bundle holds.

''Proves'': the first operator has recorded the node's custody state and holds recovery material, the card and the bundle, apart from the running machine.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/threshold-attest-recovery`>>

!! Recovery cards and the recovery contact signing boundary

`persona new` splits no root by default. `persona new <index> --name '<label>' --split-root` opts into the 2-of-3 memory split. No card restores a charter. Recovery contacts and paper restore a face's key lineage; only key material restores its private documents (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-policy#/distinct-recovery`>>).

`persona new` prints no recovery material by default; `--split-root` opts into self-custody reconstruction material and prints its own warning about shares. `lares persona recovery-contact show <index>` reads a face's prefix and set. `lares persona recovery-contact offer --prefix <prefix>` cuts one recovery contact key for that face; the recovery contact keeps its card and hands over its offer. `lares persona recovery-contact enrol <index> --threshold <k> <offer>…` lets the face's current key sign its typed first enrolment. The vessel keeps that set in a sealed `recovery-contact-set` record and refuses a key another face holds. A later recovery contact rotation needs the recovery contact quorum; the root-card reader and the bundle's carriage of an enrolled set still stand owed.

▢ ''OWED — the catastrophe rite.'' A lost card at a two-seat Nexus ends that Nexus (#/reserve holds the cold-key rite). `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road` and `kahu.mem#/seed-and-nexus` track charter replacement and Nexus founding.

''Do'': compare each physical or offline copy with the CLI's printed destination instructions, then confirm an independent second location.

''See'': every required holder can locate the material assigned to them without access to the running node.

''Proves'': recovery material has an independent custody path. The CLI cannot prove that a human placed a card safely.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/household-reach`>>

!! 7 · Establish household reach

Household HTTPS uses a DNS-01 wildcard certificate for a Kahu-held domain and one split-horizon resolver. The resolver answers each household name from one records file the operator keeps, one `<LAN address> <name>` line per machine (`LARES_DNS_RECORDS`, mode 0600 owned by the vessel's uid; `docker-compose.surface.yml` names how to check an edit and restart the resolver). Write each address plainly, with no octet led by a zero. A public record under the household zone stays unreachable from inside the house. mDNS offers a hint only. No device installs a certificate authority. Keep the DNS credential and TLS private key outside the vessel state and image.

''When one phone cannot reach the household name.'' The household name answers only from the household's own resolver, the one the router hands every device. A phone or a browser set to send its DNS questions somewhere else (encrypted DNS) never asks that resolver, so the household name fails on that device alone while it works on the others. At home, set the device back to the network's own DNS:

* ''Android'': Settings → Network & internet → Private DNS (on some phones: Connections → More connection settings → Private DNS). Choose ''Automatic'' or ''Off''. A named provider (a hostname typed there) skips the household resolver. The setting holds for every network the phone joins, so a person who wants a named provider away from home turns it back on when they leave.
* ''iPhone and iPad'': Settings → Wi-Fi → the ⓘ beside the household network → turn off ''iCloud Private Relay'' for that network alone. A DNS or VPN app, or an installed DNS profile, sends questions past the household resolver as well: pause it at home, or remove the profile under Settings → General → VPN & Device Management.
* ''Chrome'': Settings → Privacy and security → (Security →) Use secure DNS (the path differs a little between a phone and a computer) → ''With your current service provider'', or off. A named provider skips the household resolver.
* ''Firefox'': Settings → Privacy & Security → DNS over HTTPS → ''Default Protection'' or ''Off''. ''Increased'' or ''Max'' protection with a named provider skips the household resolver.

''See'': on that device, at home, the household name opens the vessel's page over `https` with no certificate warning. Away from home the household name answers nothing, by design.

▢ ''OWED — household HTTPS integration.'' The optional Compose profile supplies a terminator and split-horizon resolver. The operator still needs DNS-01 issuance and renewal, LAN binding and DHCP wiring, and a verified trusted origin on a household device. Carriers: `harbor.mem`, `secure-context.mem`, and `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road`.

''See'': the carriers describe the required DNS-01 wildcard, resolver, and terminator boundary; the road still tracks the integration.

''Proves'': the operator can distinguish the required design from a finished household service.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/gather-the-fleet`>>

!! 8 · Add the first operator's browser, then tablet or phone leaves

The first operator starts with a browser leaf; a tablet or phone forms a separate leaf. Each device mints its own key and receives one face's fleet-join answer for that key. The answer transfers no seed; it carries the internal `device-admit` edge, that face's certificates and grants, and its sealed address secret.

▢ ''OWED — browser and phone leaf ceremony.'' Browser crossing and render rungs run, and a leaf-side edit survives a reload. Fleet join runs on node and browser. The complete household phone walk still needs secure-origin boot, on-device key minting and a verified live leaf. Do not use a desktop seed on a phone or call an HTTP LAN page a secure context. Measure actual device capabilities with `tools/capability-probe/`. Carriers: `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road` (browser rungs and HARBOR), `secure-context.mem`, and `persona-circle.mem`.

''See'': no supported command sequence can yet show a household phone leaf online.

''Proves'': the runbook marks the actual boundary and keeps the per-device-key law intact.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/second-operator`>>

!! 9 · The second operator founds a seed, then both found the Nexus

The second operator founds a separate SEED on their own machine (steps 2, 5 and 6), with their own state, persona, custody, seat and device keys. `lares nexus seal cofound open --release <manifest> --out <offer>` mints the first founder's seat and cadence keys and prints a cold successor card for read-back. Carry the offer file to the second vault. There, `lares nexus seal cofound join <offer> --out <join>` asks for the first seat's fingerprint as heard, mints the second founder's seat and cadence keys, prints its card, and half-signs the joint inception. Carry the join file back. The first founder runs `lares nexus seal cofound seal <join> --out <inception>`, types the second fingerprint as heard, signs, and files the charter. The second founder runs `lares nexus seal cofound seal <inception>` and files the same charter under the same AID. One signed cadence inception rides the joint founding; each hearth signs it. Each seed keeps `founding: null` and its own AID.

Each founder then consents to their own carriage contract. `lares nexus contract <nym> --nexus <aid> --out <file>` opens a partial; the other vault runs `lares nexus contract --from <file> --out <grown-file>` to judge and cosign it. Carry the grown file back so the opener lands the act too. `lares nexus revoke <nym> --nexus <aid> --out <file>` takes the same two-vault path. Each receiving vault judges against its own charter head and board before signing and again before landing. The independent two-root import, boot and restart witness still stands owed, along with the kapae and roll-anchor doors and later multi-chair rolls.

!!! Carriage into one household's seed (built)

This path admits the second operator as a carrier of the FIRST operator's household seed; it founds no Nexus and moves no phase. Neither operator gains a seat in the other's charter. Transfer every public file between workspaces with integrity intact.

''Do'': the second operator follows steps 2 and 5–6 on their own machine. The first operator exports the charter.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh nexus seal export --no-json > "$LARES_WORKSPACE/founding-roster.mem"
```

Transfer `founding-roster.mem` to the second operator's workspace without changing its bytes. The second operator imports the first operator's charter and accepts carriage for its Nexus AID.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh nexus seal import --carry /workspace/founding-roster.mem
tools/lares-container.sh nexus accept-carriage --nexus <nexus-aid>
```

The second operator gives the printed Nym and contract signature to the first operator. The first operator contracts that Nym and exports the resulting admit bundle.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh nexus contract <nym> --sig <contract-signature> --json > "$LARES_WORKSPACE/admit-bundle.json"
tools/lares-container.sh nexus members --list
```

Transfer `admit-bundle.json` to the second operator's workspace without changing its bytes. The second operator takes that bundle.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh nexus admit-take /workspace/admit-bundle.json
```

''See'': the second operator receives a signed contract-in; the first operator's member list names the second operator after the contract act.

''Proves'': both households retain independent roots and the first household seed records the second operator's carriage membership; no Nexus founding occurs.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/hosted-family-member`>>

!! 10 · Host a family member's phone leaf

A further family member may ride as a user leaf under an operator's Nexus. That path still needs the same secure browser origin and per-device key property. It does not turn the family member into a second operator or transfer an operator seed.

▢ ''OWED — hosted phone-browser admission.'' The CLI supplies host and walker invite verbs. The complete phone-browser path still needs trusted HTTPS, on-device key minting, admission, and a verified live leaf. Carriers: `membership-doctrine.mem` and `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road`.

''See'': membership doctrine describes the user relation; the road tracks browser and foreign-leaf work still ahead.

''Proves'': the runbook distinguishes hosted family membership from operator-to-operator carriage.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/aftercare`>>

!! 11 · Aftercare and the next road

Keep house-root and `/workspace` backups distinct. The house-root backup holds vessel homes and custody; the workspace holds the editable corpus. Keep the custody passphrase or paper pin away from both. Renew the DNS-01 certificate through the operator-owned renewal route when that path lands. Keep the host shim and its hooks on the host; keep vessel code in the image.

A live vessel may continue with its local corpus while household HTTPS, browser leaves, or user admission remain owed. The road carries these build steps and the two-household live check. The sensorium runbook carries a separate later walk.

''Do'': record backup owners and locations, then follow `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road` for the remaining HARBOR and two-household work.

''See'': the operator can name the next owner and carrier for every owed slice.

''Proves'': the first node remains legible and recoverable while the household path advances.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/carry-across-refound`>>

!! 11a · Restore the house from its keep

A re-founding restores the house from its keep, at one revision: the keep (`lares rite keep`, below) holds the house's last cut as a bundle under `house/` of the operator's own private repo (its content in clear, its roster of faces sealed to the house's keep pair, whose secret rides beside it in `keep-seat.json` wrapped under the steward's passphrase and paper sheet), and a brand-new house founds at a fresh root, `~/.lares-next`, beside the house it replaces, imports that keep from the workspace it rests in, reads par, and swaps into `~/.lares` by rename. The face's memory crosses as content onto the new face that carries the same pet-name. Both houses stand on the one image the checkout builds. The pūlama stands after the swap, on the renamed root, with the first keep after it: it binds `$LARES_HOUSE/hearth` and `$LARES_HOUSE/pulama/state` by path and restarts unless stopped, so a pūlama lit at `~/.lares-next` would keep binding a path the rename empties.

''Do'': in this order, each line on the operator's real terminal, PASTED ONE LINE AT A TIME (zsh reads a pasted block as one buffer, so one bad line stops them all). Every line pastes as written: no angle-bracket placeholder stands in one. Set the names it needs first, one `NAME=value` line each, before any line that reads them: `PET0`, `PET1`, `PET2` (each face's pet-name), `CHAIR0` (face 0's chair label), `K` (a pending-lift count its lay printed); a line that reads a name unset refuses by name (`${PET0:?}`) and never runs on a default. A line that ends `# each face` runs once per face: set `N=0`, `N=1`, `N=2` and `PET` to that face's pet-name, then paste it. The census files rest outside both house roots (`~/census-drill`), so the rename moves none of them. Where the standing house cannot answer (a lost disk, a torn hearth), skip its census and its keep: the keep the remote already holds restores it, and the census of the new house after the import stands as the record. The found and `persona new` lines take the pet-names and chair labels `persona list` reads on the standing house, since the bundle's roster rests sealed; with no standing house the steward names them from its own record, and the import's preview names each bundle pet-name no face here carries (`carry-face-unmatched`) and each chair label that differs (`carry-redeclare-diverged`). Each import asks for the steward's passphrase or paper sheet the old house's keep sealed under, opens the keep seat the bundle carries, then the roster; no key file crosses by hand. Three acts stay distinct. A kukulu restand stands the vessel onto a new image and keeps the hearth, clearing nothing; it stays the default way the house changes. A re-found is the rare, breaking path, where a change in the house's form reaches past what a kukulu restand carries forward, and it clears `hearth/` alone: done in place, it lays back from the house's own projections (the face pools and realm trees are git trees, and every mirror seeds back), with no keep and no clone; done onto a new root or a new machine, which this walk covers, it binds the keep as a workspace the operator owns, the keep's own checkout or a clone the operator makes in their own space outside the house root, and imports from `/lar/workspaces/house-backup/house`; no copy of the keep ever rests inside `config/`. The import reads that path as it reads any bundle directory (`verifyBundle` over the resolved path, with no workspace refusal in the way); a drill of this exact bound-workspace import stands owed. The new house's first unlock seats its own keep pair under the passphrase it reads, so its keeps seal to that pair.

```sh
# THE STANDING HOUSE (~/.lares): no LARES_HOUSE in the environment, so the env file names ~/.lares
tools/lares-container.sh vault unlock --all --face 0 && tools/lares-container.sh persona list   # each face's pet-name and chair label: set PET0 PET1 PET2 CHAIR0 from it, one line each
tools/lares-container.sh wear $N && node tools/census-drill.mjs census --root ~/.lares --label "old $PET" --out ~/census-drill/old-$PET.json --lares tools/lares-container.sh   # each face
lares rite keep --workspace house-backup --face 0               # on the host, as the steward: the last cut, pushed and landed
lares rite receipts --face 0                                     # conferre reads the keep's tessera par
tools/lares-container.sh down                                    # never accept-loss: a census that asks for it means vault unlock, let it settle, down again
# THE NEW HOUSE: export LARES_HOUSE="$HOME/.lares-next" in this shell, for every line through the swap's down
mkdir -p ~/.lares-next/config && cp -a ~/.lares/sensoriums ~/.lares/library ~/.lares-next/
tools/lares-container.sh found --hearth --name "${PET0:?}" --chair "${CHAIR0:?}"   # the names persona list read on the standing house
# re-state the temp consent by hand: projection.temp and projection.tempExpiryDays from ~/.lares/config/config.json into ~/.lares-next/config/config.json
tools/lares-container.sh up --workspace house-backup=<the keep checkout, as the standing house binds it>   # up unlocks on the attached terminal; the keep's last cut rests there, read where it lies
tools/lares-container.sh persona new 1 --name "${PET1:?}"   # asks to bind a route of its own: type the same passphrase
tools/lares-container.sh persona new 2 --name "${PET2:?}"   # the same passphrase again
tools/lares-container.sh vault unlock --all --face 1             # each face stands lit in a composition once
tools/lares-container.sh wear $N && tools/lares-container.sh vessel lay --apply --yes   # each face: canon first, then that face's mirrors
tools/lares-container.sh vessel lay --apply --lift-kahua "${K:?}"    # still wearing that face, where its lay said `kahua: K item(s) stand pending`
tools/lares-container.sh wear $N && node tools/census-drill.mjs census --root ~/.lares-next --label "new-base $PET" --out ~/census-drill/new-base-$PET.json --lares tools/lares-container.sh   # each face, before the import
tools/lares-container.sh wear 0 && tools/lares-container.sh import /lar/workspaces/house-backup/house   # a preview: asks for the steward's passphrase or paper sheet, writes nothing; every pet-name maps to a face
tools/lares-container.sh wear $N && tools/lares-container.sh import /lar/workspaces/house-backup/house --apply   # each face; each import asks for the passphrase or paper sheet again
tools/lares-container.sh wear $N && tools/lares-container.sh import /lar/workspaces/house-backup/house --apply && tools/lares-container.sh vessel lay --apply --yes   # each face again: lands 0
tools/lares-container.sh wear $N && node tools/census-drill.mjs census --root ~/.lares-next --label "new $PET" --out ~/census-drill/new-$PET.json --lares tools/lares-container.sh   # each face
node tools/census-drill.mjs compare --old ~/census-drill/old-*.json --new ~/census-drill/new-[!b]*.json --base ~/census-drill/new-base-*.json --json --out ~/census-drill/parity.json   # par
tools/lares-container.sh lei kui --rite keep --branch house-backup --returns 12 --face 0   # before any pūlama: a ward act, which a pūlama refuses `needs-the-human`; the lei door names its face, since several faces stand open
# THE SWAP
tools/lares-container.sh down
unset LARES_HOUSE && tools/lares-container.sh pulama down   # only where a pūlama stands on the old house: it binds ~/.lares/hearth by path, so it stops before the rename
cp ~/.lares/lares-surface.env ~/.lares-next/ && mv -T ~/.lares ~/.lares-prior-$(date +%F) && mv -T ~/.lares-next ~/.lares   # the env file comes from the house being moved, never from a prior; mv -T refuses an existing target
tools/lares-container.sh up                                      # the renamed house, the workspace bound at /lar/workspaces/house-backup
tools/lares-container.sh wear $N && node tools/census-drill.mjs census --root ~/.lares --label "swap $PET" --out ~/census-drill/swap-$PET.json --lares tools/lares-container.sh   # each face
node tools/census-drill.mjs compare --old ~/census-drill/new-[!b]*.json --new ~/census-drill/swap-*.json --json --out ~/census-drill/swap.json   # par against the census before the swap
# THE LIT HOUSE: ~/.lares/lares-surface.env carries LARES_PULAMA=1
tools/lares-container.sh pulama up                               # standing unlit
tools/lares-container.sh pulama light                            # the operator's own route lights every face
tools/lares-container.sh up                                      # the vessel seats its holder over the socket and names what the pūlama waits on
tools/lares-container.sh pulama admit                            # no face named: the standing image serves every lit face
tools/lares-container.sh vault unlock --all --face 0             # gathers no route: the vessel stands unlocked, served by the pūlama
tools/lares-container.sh kahua get --face N --json              # each face answers its own view
lares rite keep --workspace house-backup --face 0               # on the host, as the steward whose line holds the lei: the restored house's first keep
lares rite receipts --face 0                                     # conferre reads the keep's tessera par
```

''Where an answer departs from the road.'' The parity's compare exits 0 on `par` and 1 naming each difference; both houses stand at one revision, so a row the import stood off reads red there too, and the operator settles it by hand (`lares tiddler get --meme`, then `lares meme put` with its `--base`) before the swap. An import whose route opens no slot of the bundle's keep seat refuses `keep-route-refused` before anything moves: present the passphrase or paper sheet the old house's keep sealed under. An import with no terminal to ask refuses `keep-route-absent`. A bundle that carries no `keep-seat.json`, or one whose seat reads no keep pair, refuses `bundle-keep-seat-shape`: keep again on the standing house, pull the clone, and import that keep. Every line up to the swap's `down` leaves `~/.lares` as it stood, so a rollback there runs `down`, `unset LARES_HOUSE` and `up` on the standing house. After the swap the rollback runs `down` (and `pulama down` once the pūlama stands), `mv -T ~/.lares ~/.lares-next`, `mv -T ~/.lares-prior-$(date +%F) ~/.lares` (the dated name the swap printed, when the rollback runs another day), then `up`; the prior house stands whole until the operator burns it.

''When the standing house cannot answer.'' A house that gives no census and cuts no fresh keep (faceless, its faces locked past opening) restores from its projections, which stay plain on disk: `projections/` holds each face's root by pet-name and every realm tree as a git tree. The honest path founds at the new root from a COPY (`cp -a ~/.lares/projections ~/.lares/sensoriums ~/.lares/library ~/.lares-next/`, the old root only read), runs `found` and `up` there, mints each face with `persona new N --name` (one passphrase for all; `persona new 1` and `persona new 2` each ask to bind a route, and the same passphrase answers), then lays each face by its pet-name: `wear N`, then `vessel lay --apply --yes`, the steward first (house canon waits on face 0's wear). With no keep to import, that lay is the restore; the census of the new house stands as the record, and the swap runs by rename as below. Read each face's PersonaGroup id off `projections/face-labels/*.json` joined to `kahua-index.toml` (the newest label per pet-name is the new face), since `persona list` prints no group id; the seat then admits h1's group with `nexus cabal admit` and re-states each share by hand, since no old `config/share.tid` crosses. A prior house keeps a DATED name (`~/.lares-prior-$(date +%F)`) and the swap's `mv -T` refuses an existing target: an older `~/.lares-prior` would take the moved house INSIDE it as `~/.lares-prior/.lares`, silently, and `cp` of its env file would carry a stale env that lacks `LARES_PULAMA`, `LARES_KEEP_WORKSPACE` and `LARES_WORKSPACES`. The pūlama still runs the old image after the swap until `lares rite kukulu` re-stands it, and the new house holds no kukulu lei: string one first (`lares lei kui --rite kukulu --branch develop --returns 6 --all`).

''The drill proves the restore.'' `tools/house-keep.test.mjs` walks the same road on two scratch houses: the keep pushes to a scratch remote, a brand-new house imports a fresh clone, a clone with one flipped byte refuses `bundle-hash-mismatch` before anything moves, and the parity reads par.

''The keep wakes each wiki itself.'' The vessel holds a small budget of rotatable wiki pins, so a house of more shared wikis than that leaves some cold at any one moment. The keep's cut (`rite keep-cut`) holds each wiki the house lists in turn and hands the pins back, so a page put into a cold wiki travels. A plain `lares export` reads the pins as they stand: a wiki no island mounts at the cut reads as the named omission `cold-wikis`, its working rows travelling without their override bases; `lares wiki hold <slug>`, wearing each face that wrote there, wakes it first.

''The house keeps itself.'' A workspace named `house-backup` binds a clone of the operator's own private repo (`LARES_WORKSPACES=house-backup=<clone dir>` in the surface env, so every `up` binds it at `/lar/workspaces/house-backup`), and a committed `.gitattributes` holding `house/** -text` stands in the clone (`<into>/** -text` where `--into` names another place; `house` carries the default). The keep lei grants the rite for that workspace: strung by hand as above, or by a yes at `vault unlock`, which offers it under the passphrase the unlock already read wherever its own process reads `LARES_KEEP_WORKSPACE` (or the one `LARES_WORKSPACES` pair) naming the workspace and no keep lei with a return left stands. Through the host shim the unlock runs inside the vessel, whose compose carries both names from the surface env (at the `up` after they land there), and it strings the lei as the face the unlock names, else as the daemon mount, naming that face; under a pūlama the unlock gathers no route, so on a restore the hand strings the lei before the pūlama stands. `lares rite keep` runs on the host, as the face `--face N` names, else as the daemon mount (it says "acting as hN, the daemon mount", and a vessel at the floor refuses `keep-face-unmounted`): it guards the clone (a branch with a remote, the `-text` guard, nothing changed outside `house/` and this device's `devices/<name>-<id8>/`), mirrors this device's Claude Code sessions into that device dir and scans the mirror for secret-shaped strings, has the vessel cut the same bundle `export` cuts into `house/` (its roster sealed to the keep pair's public half, so the cut asks for no route) and spend one return, scans the bundle too, commits `keep <bundle id>` (the house and the sessions in the one commit), rebases, pushes, and lands the tessera at the bundle id the pushed manifest names; `lares rite receipts` then reads that tessera par. `tools/lares-container.sh down` makes the host call `lares rite keep --if-due`, which names no face and so keeps as the daemon mount, while the vessel stands (`LARES_HOST_CLI` names the host's door), and a keep that refuses never blocks the down; the wake prints the keep line, naming the lei's returns or the cure for a keep that cut and never landed. A push that fails lands nothing and names its cure: mend the remote and keep again. `lares rite keep --if-due` keeps only where the house moved since the bundle the remote already holds, so a house and sessions that did not move spend nothing and ask no remote. `--no-sessions` (or `LARES_KEEP_SESSIONS=0` in the surface env) keeps the house alone; the sessions mirror and scan before any return spends, so a secret-shaped string refuses with the return unspent; with the vessel down nothing commits or pushes, and the mirror waits in the clone for the keep after the `up`. The device id stays the one `~/.config/claude-backup/device.json` holds. The keep pair seats at the first `vault unlock` under the passphrase it reads, at `config/keep-seat.json`, which a re-founding's reset never reaches; a paper sheet read back at `vault bind --paper` joins it as a second slot. A house where no pair rests refuses the cut `keep-seat-absent` and names `lares vault unlock`. `lares home tend` seats a SessionEnd hook that runs `lares rite keep --if-due` detached, so each session's end keeps what moved; `bin/sync` retires once one real sync reads par against a keep.

''The bundle's content rests unencrypted.'' Export copies a face's private memory as plain files and says so on its closing line. Only the roster (pet-names, Handles, chair labels, share rows) rides sealed, to the keep pair, and `keep-seat.json` beside it opens under the steward's passphrase or paper sheet. It refuses a destination inside the workspace or inside the house root, save the house's config home: a reset keeps `config/`, and the container binds `/lar/config` to `$LARES_HOUSE/config`, so the bundle rests where the host reads it (the vault backup's own precedent). Keep it on a disk only the operator holds, and remove it once the import reads back.

''A carry act follows the document.'' A lay lays the worn face's daemon memory, and names every other open face's as waiting for its own wear (below). A wiki's workbench lays ONCE, for its document, at the wear of a face that holds a writer's seat there (or of the face that stands the wiki first); a lay run under any other face names the folder as waiting ("the workbench of <slug> lays once, at the wear of a face that holds a writer's seat there") and exits 0, and none of its files reach that face's layers. A shared wiki re-stands at its owner's wear, the owner read by pet-name off the share share entry, and the lay's closing act lands the shares again. Only a face's own `personal` layer of a wiki asks that face's wear ("stands unworn, and a face's own layer of a wiki lays at that face's wear"): wear it (`lares wear N`; `vault unlock --face N` lights a shut face and, on an unlocked vessel, leaves the worn face where it stood) and lay again.

''Lay comes before import.'' The walk runs found -> up -> lay -> import. The lay lays the checkout's canon; the import's bag records then meet it at the same `INGEST` three-way gate and stand off where they diverge, never a silent overwrite either way. An applied import over a disk-sourced holding the vessel never laid refuses `carry-canon-unseeded` and names `vessel lay --apply`.

''Found each former face before the first lay.'' A hearth that held more than one face keeps each face's root under `projections/faces/<pet name>/`, and the lay lays a root only onto the face whose pet name makes it. So the walk for such a hearth runs found -> up -> `lares persona new N --name '<the same pet-name>'` for each former face -> `lares vault unlock --all --face N` -> lay. A lay run before a former face stands again lays house canon and every root it can match, refuses each unmatched root by name (`seed-face-unmatched`, with the `persona new` line that cures it) and exits 1: that face's memory still waits for a hand, and an exit of 0 would tell a script the hearth stood restored. Found the face and lay again; the second lay lands that face alone.

''The lay lays every mirror back into its one layer.'' After canon, the lay reads the projections root: each face's working folder (`faces/<pet name>/wikis/daemon`) into that face's daemon working layer (a kāpae shadow raises as a kāpae), its canon folder (`faces/<pet name>/bags/daemon`) into its daemon canon, each house bag into its canon, each other wiki's one folder into its workbench, each face's `faces/<pet name>/personal/<slug>` into that face's own personal layer of the wiki, through the one `INGEST` three-way gate: a file equal to its base lands nothing, a moved one lands, and one whose records moved too stands off at the shore within that one layer. A second lay lands nothing. Canon never writes working; the top layer always shadows, and where canon moved past a copy's base the lay warns and writes nothing. `projections/ashes/` stays out of every walk.

''The face root carries across by pet-name.'' A face names itself when it is created (`lares found --hearth --name`, `lares persona new N --name`), and its root takes that pet name: `projections/faces/<pet name>/`. A relation id changes at every re-found; the pet name does not, so after reset -> found the re-minted face under the same pet name finds its root where it stands, and the lay lays it in place: nothing adopts, copies or moves. A root lands on the ONE face whose pet name makes it, as an import maps a bundle face: none, two, or a face whose pet name meets another's without case refuse THAT root by name and land nothing of it, and the lay goes on for every other root. Each face's label (`projections/face-labels/<relation>.json`, written whenever a face stands) names the pet name a relation wore and rests beside `faces/`, never inside it. So a tear-out with no export recovers from disk alone. A rename moves the face's root.

''Each face's memory lands at its own wear.'' Unlock every face your one passphrase opens (`lares vault unlock --all --face 1`), then lay once wearing each face in turn: the worn face's working and face-canon folders land in its own layer through the ordinary wave. Every other open face's ride an INGEST that names it, which the island lands in that face's own document only where it can open that document, never on the worn face (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/the-lit-set`>>); on a walked hearth a sibling's daemon layer rests sealed under its own key and answers `face-unresolved` or `document-unavailable`, so the lay names that face as waiting (`stands open, and its own layer lays at its own face's wear`), lands nothing of it anywhere and exits 0. Its kahua items wait the same way (`lifts at its own face's wear`). A face that stays SHUT waits by name (`<pet-name> stays shut: lares vault unlock --face N, then lay again`), its root standing as it stands until its own lay lands it, and it never blocks the rest; unlock it and lay again, and that face lands alone. The import reads the same. No face's memory ever lands in another face. House canon alone waits on the worn face: it lands only in a lay the steward (face 0) wears, whichever faces stand open beside it.

''Share a bag by name.'' A custom bag stays its owner's; `lares bag share notes --read <referee-pet-name> --write <steward-pet-name>` shares it by pet-name (`hN` and `--face N` resolve to one), writes `config/share.tid`, and lands it by reference and delegation while both faces stand lit; `lares bag share --list` reads who reads and writes each bag (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/the-share-config`>>). House canon takes no share entry: no share lifts a write into it.

''House canon: the steward writes it, every other face reads the floor.'' Every disk-sourced holding but the public board (`lares`, `lararium`, `sdm`, `elyncia`, `elyncia-referee`; `crossroads` stays the Nexus's board) rests in the Kahu realm at contract and takes no catalog entry, so no face can fork it (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/cap-tier#/house-canon`>>). The Kahu steward (h0) writes it live: the founding lands each bag in the realm, and the steward's boot lands a bag the checkout gained since. Every other face reads a `public`-tier holding (`lares`, `lararium`, `sdm`, `elyncia`) as the floor snapshot every DreamNet user receives by CID, and the contract-tier `elyncia-referee` through its realm document and a read grant, since the public floor carries `public`-tier bags alone; a hearth founded earlier sheds the referee snapshot at its next `lares nexus release <file> --cut`, the slug named in `dropped`. Its lay reads clean and names each such holding `house canon, written by the Kahu steward (h0); this face reads the floor`, and an edit there copies up into that wiki's working layer. Admitting another face to write canon stays the operator's deliberate act: `lares nexus cabal admit <group>` at the seat quorum.

''The vessel moves its floor by the hand.'' The VESSEL pins the floor, one pin per Nexus, in its control home; a face pins nothing, so wearing another face moves no floor. `lares vessel floor status` names the pinned release, the newest the board shows, whether it fast-forwards along `prior`, and any fork; `lares vessel floor accept [<release>]` moves the pin to a descendant, refuses a fork or a release that does not descend, and either swaps the floor live or raises the reboot-pending alert in each live wiki. Every island mounts the pinned floor when it boots, the daemon island (which answers `tiddler get … --bag <slug>` and `meme list --bag <slug>`) as each wiki's: a wear composes the islands again, so after an accept every face reads the released canon from its next wear on, with no restart, and a restart reads it too; the composition that stood at the accept keeps the prior floor until then (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/w26-floor-lane#/the-vessel-pin`>>).

''Kahua lifts by the operator's hand, with eyes.'' The lay previews each kahua item it would lift (face, title, body digest; `differs` where a standing head says otherwise), and lifts them under the new face's grant only on a typed count at the terminal or `--lift-kahua <N>` naming that count; a bare `--yes` lifts none, and a wrong count refuses before anything writes. Items left unconfirmed stand pending: `lares shore` lists them and `lares shore lift-kahua --count <N>` lifts them later.

''Up and unlock come before import.'' `up` on a terminal runs the unlock itself; only an `up` with no terminal attached names `vault unlock` for a terminal afterwards. The import lands each face's memory in that face's own root, `projections/faces/<pet name>/`, for the face the standing, unlocked vessel reads; on a vessel that stands down or locked it refuses `carry-face-unworn` and names `up` and `vault unlock`.

''A burned face's root goes to ashes by the hand.'' The reset keeps `projections/` (survive-refound ruling), and with it every face's cleartext root. A root whose pet name no standing face carries stays where it stands, and the next export refuses `carry-unclaimed-face-folder` and names it: found that face again, rename the face that should hold it, move the root out of the house root, or pass `--accept-omissions`. Ashes (`projections/ashes/<pet name>/`) take a burned root alone, never one whose pet name still stands: plaintext still, out of the live mirror set, never read back and never projected into.

''The override base travels.'' A working copy's base (the carrier identity, `carrierHash` tagged `sha256:<hex>`, of the canon record it forked from) rests beside the face label (`projections/face-labels/<relation>.bases.json`), kept by the vessel off that face's own working slots; the lay stamps each base back on the copy it lays, and the bundle's working record carries the same base for the import, so canon's fresher-than-the-copy warning still reads after found -> up -> lay, with or without an export.

''See'': `import --apply` prints each wave's tallies; the AI's memories read back through MCP (`tiddler_get(meme=True)`) on the new face.

''Proves'': a face's authored memory survives a re-founding as content, under a new relation and a new grant.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-three-face-rite`>>

!! 11b · The three-face dogfood rite

One human, three faces, one passphrase: the steward `joshua-fontany` (face 0, the Kahu chair), the engineer (face 1) and the referee (face 2). The rite stands the hearth, writes memory, shares a wiki, moves canon through the offering door, crosses a browser in, and carries everything across a re-founding. Run it on a span head CI holds green, after `tools/lares-container.sh build` stamps the image at that head and `$LARES_HOUSE/hearth` stands empty.

''Stand.''

```sh
lares found --hearth --name 'joshua-fontany' --chair 'dreamnet-kahu-0'   # the veil floor first, then face 0 lands over it; its seed charter AID names the hearth
lares up                                                                  # up unlocks on the attached terminal; the steward (face 0) stands lit and the session wears it
lares vessel lay --apply --yes                                           # the steward's seed: house canon into the Kahu realm, then every mirror
lares vessel floor status                                                 # the pin reads the genesis floor
lares persona new 1 --name 'joshua-fontany-engineer'                      # the same passphrase; no --seat (the engineer holds no Kahu chair)
lares persona new 2 --name 'joshua-fontany-referee'                       # the same passphrase
lares vault unlock --all --face 1                                         # lights all three; the session wears the engineer
lares vault unlock --face 0                                               # the steward again: the share below lands under the face that owns the workbench
lares wiki share lares --write joshua-fontany-engineer --read joshua-fontany-referee   # seats the two faces on the `lares` workbench
```

''The steward shares the house wiki.'' Once the faces exist and each has stood lit in a composition (the wear above), the steward seats them on the `lares` wiki's workbench by an explicit share. The share entry names faces by pet-name and rests in vessel-private places alone (`config/share.tid`, the control document), with the workbench's owner beside it (`owner`); the realm's roster lists each seated hive flat, and seating one's own second face writes the same record as seating a stranger's. The door answers `workbench: wiki lares rides realm … (2 member face(s))`. ''The rest gate'': this vessel seals the `lares` working document under the steward's own key, so the door also says that each seated face HERE writes its own `personal` layer of `lares` and offers from there, until a workbench's rest seals for its roster (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-workbench`>>, the rest gate). The seats stand for the wire and for each face's own vessels.

''The steward lays first.'' House canon takes the Kahu steward's hand, and the Kahu predicate reads the face the session wears, so the lay that lays canon runs while the session wears face 0: here, straight after `up`. A lay worn as any other face names each house canon holding `house canon, written by the Kahu steward (h0); this face reads the floor`, lands none of it and reads clean, so a hearth laid only as the engineer holds empty canon documents and its release cut refuses `floor-cut-thin`. Every holding the lay feeds stands under the canon source BEFORE the vessel boots: the house canon seats once, at boot, and a holding that lands there afterwards refuses `house-canon-unseated` at the lay and mints nothing; run `lares down && lares up` (the steward's boot lands it in the realm), then lay again. `lares vessel floor status` answers once the vessel has stood once; on a store no boot has touched it refuses `floor-no-genesis`.

''Memory.'' The `lares` MCP server runs inside the standing container (`docker compose exec`), so `/mcp` connects only after `up`. Reconnect `/mcp`, and the AI writes memories into the worn face's daemon wiki: `meme_put` with `recipe="default"`, each stage meme under `lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/<class>/<slug>` (a path that reads no one or two lowercase segments refuses `stage-path-shape`; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/kahua-memory-stage#/the-hearth-lane`>>). The seat names every refusal in the refusal's own words.

''The lift.'' The AI stages a meme for the fleet (the MCP tool `kahua_stage`, or `lares kahua stage <title> --face 1 --text …`), and the stage answers the ROW digest (the body and the carried fields). The hand lifts it at a terminal: `lares kahua lift <title> --face 1 --taint none` prints the staged meme, lifts only on that row digest's first 8 hex typed back (a bare `--yes` lifts nothing), and then ALWAYS asks the face's passphrase, since a lift signs under the face's root; `--digest <hex>` spares the typed-back digest alone, never the passphrase. Where the AI proposed first (`kahua_propose`, or `lares kahua propose`), the lift names it with `--proposal <intent-id>`: a proposal carries the BODY digest (the body bytes alone), which the lift compares with the staged body, so the two digests never need to match each other. The lift unions the proposal's taint with `--taint`.

''Wearing another face.'' To write as the referee: `lares vault unlock --face 2`, then reconnect `/mcp`. With no pūlama, the wear asks the passphrase again at a terminal, although the face already stands open: no wear-only door stands there. Under a pūlama a session wears any lent face with no route (the lit house, step 5).

''A shared workbench.''

```sh
lares wiki init notes
lares wiki share notes --write joshua-fontany-engineer --read joshua-fontany-referee --face 0
lares wiki share notes --list
```

A share's landing may outrun the verb's local wait; the verb then reads its own receipt by request id until the vessel answers, and names the landing's outcome. Write from two faces and watch one merged working layer: `lares meme put … --recipe notes` answers the layer it landed in (`layer: working` for a seated writer, `layer: personal` for the referee, who reads the workbench and writes its own personal layer; a face the share entry leaves unnamed holds no recipe entry of `notes` at all). One folder answers the workbench, `<projections>/realms/notes/wikis/notes` (the realm's default pet name, its slug, minted at its first mount; `lares realm rename notes --to <pet>` names it otherwise and `lares realm list` reads it), whichever seated writer wears. A wiki that stands cold projects a put to disk only once it wakes (`lares wiki hold notes` by a seated writer: both writers' pages then rest in the one folder, and a put made while the wiki stood cold reaches the folder at that wake); read the meme back through `lares tiddler get … --recipe notes --meme --all` before trusting the folder.

''Offer, promote, release, accept.'' Wearing the engineer, write the record as the `lares` wiki (`lares meme put <uri> --recipe lares --file …`). The answer names where it landed: `layer: personal` on this vessel (the rest gate above), mirrored to the engineer's own face root `<projections>/faces/<pet name>/personal/lares`, and never into the checkout. The steward's own working copies of a checkout-sourced wiki mirror into `wikis/lares/` in the checkout, by design (the repo workspace: git tracks them), so a steward's smoke meme belongs in `notes`. Then `lares meme offer <uri> --bag lares`: the offer reads the face's view above canon (its `personal` layer over the workbench it may read, named in the answer's `source`), carries the meme whole, its root with its slot records, and canon stays unmoved. The engineer reads house canon from the floor: `lares meme list --recipe lares`, `lares meme list --bag lararium` and `lares tiddler get … --bag lares --meme` answer each bag's floor snapshot and say so (`floor: true`), since a face outside the Kahu cabal holds no read on the live documents (its hive registers the slots of the wiki it stands and never a house canon bag, that wiki's own canon bag included); a contract-tier holding the floor never carries (`elyncia-referee`) still asks its read grant. Wearing the steward (`lares vault unlock --face 0`, the passphrase again): `lares meme offer show --bag lares`, then `lares meme offer promote <cid>`. The promotion asks the charter seat first, so a vessel that holds no seat refuses by name before canon moves; then it writes the Kahu realm's standing document and retires the offer as one outcome. The release runs three steps:

```sh
lares nexus release floor.json --cut                                      # cuts the manifest from live canon; lands nothing
lares down                                                                # the seat signs with the vessel stopped
lares nexus approve open floor.json --purpose floor-release --surface floor-release --class high-impact --out approval.json   # signs with every seat this vault holds; on this hearth it answers `ok — 1 of 1 seats`
lares up                                                                  # stand again, wearing the steward
lares nexus release floor.json --approval approval.json                   # the release lands on the publication board
```

''Stop, sign, start.'' `nexus approve open` and `nexus approve sign` open the seat's key from the sealed custody on their own road, and a standing vessel holds that custody: with the vessel up they refuse (`A sealed act rides the vessel's custody door … Stop the vessel to run it alone`). So the cut runs on the standing vessel, the vessel stops, each seat signs, the vessel stands again, and the release lands with the approval. The manifest file and the approval file rest on disk across the stop.

''One seat signs at `open`; a further seat signs into a second file.'' `approve open` signs with every seat the vault holds. The rite's hearth holds the one chair its charter counts, so `open` completes the approval alone (`ok — 1 of 1 seats`) and no `sign` runs there: a `sign` in the same vault refuses `every seat this vessel holds already signed this approval, or it holds none`. Where a charter counts more chairs than one vault holds, carry `approval.json` to each further seat's vault and sign into a NEW file: `lares nexus approve sign approval.json --confirm-statement 'ni:///sha-256;<digest>' --out approval-2.json` (the statement digest whole, as `approve open` printed it, quoted, since the `;` ends an unquoted shell word). The door never writes over the partial it read (`--out names the partial you carried … name another file`), so the carried file stays as it came if the write fails; the release then names the last file written (`--approval approval-2.json`).

The cut refuses `floor-cut-thin` by slug where a bag's live records count under half its prior snapshot's; `--thin <slug[,slug]>` names a bag that shrank on purpose. On a hearth the steward seeded, the cut answers every public-tier bag (`elyncia`, `lararium`, `lares`, `sdm`) with its live records and no `--thin`; a thin refusal there means the steward's lay has not run (see ''The steward lays first''). A floor door's refusal answers under its own name (`floor-no-charter`, `floor-no-genesis`, `floor-cut-thin`). Then `lares vessel floor accept`; every accept raises the reboot alert until a live floor swap lands.

''A browser crosses in.'' Open the page URL the banner prints: the hearth's bare origin, with no query and no fragment, and no page source reads a query. The bare arrival boots a veil with no ask. Carry the banner's floor card in at the page's carriage door (paste or file), and the anchor as its own act. To join the engineer: `lares fleet join offer --face 1 --out offer.json` → paste the offer → the page answers a request file → `lares fleet join answer request.json --out answer.json` → paste the answer. The landing founds the browser's place, so the vessel asks twice, and each ask names itself on its own button at the top of the page while the boot row reads "waiting on you": first the place card ceremony (the card written on paper and typed back), then the first passphrase ceremony. The ceremony's window closes between the two; the second ask stands on the page and the boot has not hung. The browser's veil wikis lift into the face, and the mirror opens full, styled, on the welcome landing. At any point the browser's console answers the CLI's own read verbs (`lares("help")`, `lares("vessel", "read")`, `lares("vault", "status")`): `vessel read` names the standing, the faces joined and worn, the relay, the floor pin and every ask the page still waits on. To walk the browser leg again from nothing, `lares("vessel", "reset")` or the page's "Reset this browser's vessel" button raises the reset ask; its ceremony burns only for an ask a page of this origin raised and passes the confirm gate: where the browser holds custody (a first passphrase bound, or a face joined) it asks a passphrase that opens it, and a wrong one burns nothing; at a bare floor it takes the typed words `reset this browser`. It then returns every tab of the site to the anon floor. A holder who lost every passphrase clears this site's data in the browser's own settings. (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/browser-vessel-lift`>> #/the-console-door, #/the-reset, #/the-confirm-gate).

''Carry across a re-founding'' (11a's law; this walk adds the three faces):

```sh
lares vault unlock --face 2 && lares wiki hold notes                      # the referee's wake: its own personal layer of `notes` reaches `faces/<pet name>/personal/notes`, and no page of the workbench moves
lares vault unlock --face 0 && lares wiki hold notes                      # a seated writer's wake: the workbench's pages reach `wikis/notes`, the one folder the export reads
lares export ~/.lares/config/carry-2                                      # names each wiki that still stands cold (`cold-wikis`)
lares down && lares reset
lares found --hearth --name 'joshua-fontany' --chair 'dreamnet-kahu-0'
lares up
lares persona new 1 --name 'joshua-fontany-engineer'                      # the same names, the same passphrase
lares persona new 2 --name 'joshua-fontany-referee'
lares vault unlock --all --face 1                                         # each face stands lit in a composition once
lares vault unlock --face 0 && lares vessel lay --apply --yes            # the steward's seed: house canon, each workbench it owns or writes, and the shares again; every other face's memory waits by name, and its own kahua items preview
lares vessel lay --apply --lift-kahua <N>                                # still wearing the steward: its own kahua items (the house laws among them), on the count its lay previewed
lares vault unlock --face 1 && lares vessel lay --apply --yes            # the engineer's own seed: its daemon memory, its `personal` layers, and the preview of its kahua items
lares vessel lay --apply --lift-kahua <N>                                # still wearing the engineer: its kahua items, on the count its lay previewed
lares vault unlock --face 2 && lares vessel lay --apply --yes            # the referee's own seed; where it previews kahua items of its own, lift them the same way
```

''A seated writer holds before the export.'' The export copies disk, and only a seated writer's wear writes a workbench's one folder: a face seated `read` (the referee here) designates no working mirror, so its hold wakes the wiki for its own `personal` layer alone. Each face that put a page into a wiki it never woke wakes it once, and a face that holds a writer's seat on each shared workbench (its owner, or a face the share share entry seats `write`) holds last; the export then carries the workbench's pages, and an export taken after a reader's hold alone carries none of them and names no omission for that.

''What a lay lays, and at whose wear.'' A face's private documents (its daemon memory, its `personal` layer of a wiki, its kahua stage) lay at that face's OWN wear: each rests sealed under its own face's key, so a lay worn as another face names the wait (`<pet-name> stands open, and its own layer lays at its own face's wear`, `lifts at its own face's wear`) and exits 0. A wiki's workbench lays ONCE, for its document, at the wear of a face that holds a writer's seat there; a shared wiki mints again and lays at its OWNER's wear (the share share entry names the owner by pet-name, and the wiki mints from that entry even where no page of it reached a mirror), and the lay then lands the shares again, seating each named face by pet-name. Under any other face the folder waits by name and the lay exits 0. A lay says `shares:` only where a grant or a seat moved.

''A kahua lift rides its own face's wear.'' `--lift-kahua <N>` names the count the WORN face's lay previewed, and lifts those alone; an item of another open face stays at the shore and the lay says whose wear lifts it. A lift, a stage, a withdraw or a carry named for a lent face the vessel does not mount refuses `chore-face-not-mounted` at once, naming the face asked and the mount, and the face answers once the vessel mounts it.

Read back per face: each face's memories through `/mcp`, its own personal layers of the shared wikis, and the promoted record in the steward's live canon (the canon source carried it, and the steward's lay laid it). Then `lares import ~/.lares/config/carry-2 --apply`, run wearing each face in turn, lands nothing and counts every record as `already` or, for another face's private records, `waiting`; and a final lay lands nothing and says nothing of shares.

''The floor pin starts again at genesis.'' The vessel's pin rests in its control home and the release on the former Nexus's board, and a re-founding clears both: `lares vessel floor status` answers `pinSource: genesis`, `board: absent`, and a face outside the Kahu cabal reads the promoted record `not-found` from the floor. So the release block runs once more on the new founding (`nexus release floor.json --cut`, `down`, `nexus approve open …`, `up`, `nexus release floor.json --approval approval.json`, `vessel floor accept`), and from its next wear on every face reads the promoted record on the floor (`floor: true`).

''Proves'': three faces of one human stand on one hearth, write face-private memory, share one workbench, move canon by offer and release, take a browser in by carried files, and carry all of it across a re-founding by content.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/key-replacement-cost`>>

!! Key replacement cost

Relationships acquired after founding raise the cost of replacing keys. Preserve custody before inviting another operator.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-growth-rite`>>

!! Charter succession crossing

The built `nexus seal grow` ceremony records a later charter crossing. It spans key rotation and operator signatures; this founding stops before that crossing.

''Owed, the next leap: found a hearth as an existing face'' (the operator's approval, 2026-11-12; not built). `lares vessel found` composes a place and `lares persona new 0` then mints a fresh face 0 over its floor. A person who already wears a face F on their leaves and becomes an operator needs another road: the node stands its veil floor, joins F through the carriage (the same seat door the leaves use, with the node's own seat verbs on its command line), and seals the seed charter as F. Until that road stands, a new hearth founds a new face.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/identity-classes`>>

!! Identity classes

A seat in a Nexus records a Kahu relation, held by a seat key apart from the persona root. The first operator and second operator each hold their own node identity and persona roots. Each browser device holds a device key minted on that device. A hosted family member holds a user leaf under an operator's Nexus. A carriage contract records an operator relation; a device admission records a device relation. No one class grants another class's seed.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/edges`>>

!! Edges

!!! The founding and custody surface ~ where each part reads

<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus`>> seed and Nexus readings, quorum admission and open rulings
<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/nexus-lifecycle`>> Nexus life stages, succession, liveness and quiet
<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-invite`>> hosted introductions, admission and three bound dials
<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/identity-classes`>> identity classes and their promotion paths
<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-policy`>> persona custody, recovery contacts and face rules
<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/personal-slot`>> personal slot bindings and Keyhive registration
<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/wiki-layer-ontology`>> wiki layers, working writes and canon promotion
<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/recovery-custody-scout`>> recovery custody evidence, reserve rehearsal and open work
